WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.580
When you start talking about the physical

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:02.980
world, people are like, "Ah, I can't do

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:04.420
anything about that, right?" So, they like

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:05.500
immediately tune out.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:06.780
And the whole point of this book is

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:08.220
like, here are all the things that you

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.620
can do. You don't need to be designer,

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.220
you don't need to move a wall.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:14.010
You know, you think of space is kind

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:15.450
of fixed, but it's really one of the

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:16.810
only things we can change, right?

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.170
You're not going to go back and change

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.010
your upbringing. You can't change your

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:20.770
genes.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:24.530
You can't change all these things, but you

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:27.050
can change your physical

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:27.550
surroundings.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.420
But I think that like the psychological

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.460
needs for the people who are in the

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.060
places almost come before.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.340
And just to point out that like this

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:39.140
isn't about necessarily designing buildings.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.140
It's it's more about like moving among the

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.420
like if we're not building designers, right?

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.420
We all get to choose our spaces all

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.060
the time to some extent, right?

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:50.020
I mean, you might have to go to

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:50.820
a certain office.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:52.060
You might have, you know, you've got your

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.020
house, you can't just immediately sell it.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.980
But, we have all these spaces that we

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.260
can move between, and so we actually do

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:00.580
have a lot of

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:02.740
say in these surroundings that we

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:02.910
put ourselves in.

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:04.330
Let's get into the three, right?

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:06.050
Because we've touched on agency.

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:08.410
So, these core needs

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:12.450
and the three core psychological needs

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:13.210
agency,

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:15.910
growth, and connection, right?

00:01:15.959 --> 00:01:17.800
They studied people in nursing homes and

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.280
like the one group could modify their

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:21.840
spaces, one group couldn't modify their

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:23.600
spaces. And the group that then they came

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.480
back and checked on everybody in 18 months.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.080
And the group that couldn't modify their

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.640
spaces, they're like

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.640
were they The group that could modify

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.400
their spaces were 50 per cent

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.960
more likely to still be alive.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.960
I mean, so it's again, this isn't like

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.600
I'm not saying change your space and you're

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:42.880
going to live twice as long.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:44.719
I'm just saying that like this is this

00:01:44.759 --> 00:01:46.680
is the this is how

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.760
important this need is, right?

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.440
To feel like you have a say in

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:51.800
the world around you can be like a

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:52.490
life or death thing.

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:53.890
This feeds on itself, right?

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:55.290
So, you get a little bit of agency

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:57.050
in your house, and then now you

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.850
feel like, "Okay, maybe I can take some

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:01.210
agency in the world, right?" And this is

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:02.370
I'm not a

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:05.210
you know, psychotherapist, but I you know,

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:06.810
I understand that one thing that gets

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.050
prescribed to treat depression is like clean

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.010
up your kitchen or you know, rearrange a

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:11.730
room in your house.

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:15.050
And it's not because a rearranged room makes

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.570
you less depressed, it's because the act of,

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.489
you know, feeling like exerting control or

00:02:19.529 --> 00:02:21.410
like feeling like you have a say over

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:22.650
part of your world,

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:25.650
that can spill over into other aspects of

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:26.350
your life.

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:28.830
Every company will have in the mission

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:30.830
statement something about you know, like

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:33.190
having that spirit right and we care about

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:35.390
our employees. We want them to take it

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:37.670
you know, we really that's the element of

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:40.270
a successful company but then like are the

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:43.710
spaces kind of reinforcing that message is a

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:46.150
is a question or are they working against

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:47.950
it right because if you say hey everybody's

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:49.230
equal everybody has agency.

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:51.310
We want to hear your ideas you like

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:53.430
stick certain employees in cubicles and

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:55.670
others in like nice corner offices and it's

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:57.790
like well, what's what are the spaces saying

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:01.070
about like how you know, how much you

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.110
know, control everybody actually does have

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:04.110
in the situation.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:05.740
I heard you talk on one show where

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:06.660
you're saying

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.300
that

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:09.740
one particular

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:11.540
I think it was one particular office that

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.020
was saying oh you can use the conference

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:13.780
rooms

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:16.300
if you know, if they're free just use

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:17.740
them you can do your calling there but

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:19.820
the boss was always doing his calls in

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:21.060
there so people like on oh I'm not

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:21.860
going to use it because I don't know

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.780
if he wants it or not right?

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:26.340
Right yeah and effectively like just sealed

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.420
off the conference room for all

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.540
time because the boss would just like use

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:33.100
it half an hour a day but it

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:34.020
was like

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:36.500
so and I think you know, getting back

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.260
to like, what's the general principle here?

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:41.620
It's, you know, when you're to think about

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:44.240
how is your space showing agency, right?

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:47.440
Now, Annette said, "Some office layouts

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.400
invite conversations and others kill it.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:54.240
What is the single most common and

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.160
damaging mistake organizations make in

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.840
designing workplaces and why is it so hard

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:00.560
to get right?

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:01.680
There you go."

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.880
I would say it's

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.560
the most common mistake is to jump to

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.800
some lesser priority before you think about

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:13.880
agency, growth and connection, right?

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.600
Whether it's thinking about the layout and

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.400
oh, do I want the open plan office

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:20.640
that's trendy in Silicon Valley or do I

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:23.240
want the, you know, closed private offices?

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.320
No, ask about those fundamental needs first

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:27.240
or maybe

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:30.240
it's, you know, skipping right to the paint

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.680
colors or the technology that's going to be

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:34.080
on the walls or whatever.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.120
Start with agency, competence, and

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:38.080
connection. Of course, you're eventually

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.280
going to think about those other

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:43.360
other factors, but you need to

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:45.910
begin with the most important ones.

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:48.010
Whereas if you've got a team, you've just

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:50.090
assigned a project to a team, and you

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:53.010
can say, "Okay, here's your space for the

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:54.890
project, right? The conference room in the

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:56.690
you know, you can set it up how

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:58.730
however you think is best for this project."

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:00.810
Now, all of a sudden, you're giving agency,

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:02.730
right? Cuz the people have say in their

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:05.010
space, but you're also like forcing them to

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:07.050
think through like what's this project going

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:09.330
to entail? What space is best serving this

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:09.890
project?

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:13.170
she said sharing a space, time, and

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:13.930
interest,

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:15.970
you know, like say for example, you and

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:19.090
I now are kind of sharing same space,

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:20.970
same time, same interest.

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:23.890
And she said that when people do

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:25.290
that with other people,

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:27.570
it's a it's a friendship factory.

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:29.850
Yeah. She goes, "Because, you know, you're

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:31.810
in this kind of you're actually when she

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:33.650
often does a conference or a talk, she

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:35.930
starts by saying, 'Hello, thank you for

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:38.090
coming along. We're in a shared space,

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:39.130
shared time,

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:42.250
shared interest. This is a friendship

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:43.570
factory. I'd like you to turn to the

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:45.850
person beside you before we start and tell

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:47.290
them a bit about yourself.'"

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:49.410
Right, because then now you're getting the

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:51.290
interest tie. Yeah.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:53.650
Correct. And I was thinking, you know,

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:56.010
do you think organizations should do more to

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:59.170
create friendship factories in this lonely

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:00.810
world we seem to be living in more

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:03.050
now? Definitely. I mean, it's happening

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:04.890
anyway, right? Like if you it's one of

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:06.530
these things that it's not like you can

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:07.730
choose to

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:10.250
it's happening whether you design it or

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:11.610
not, right? So, you might as well be

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:12.570
intentional about it.

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:14.010
I like this is one of the things

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:16.410
that's hilarious to me on college campuses,

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:18.450
right? It's like you we have graduation

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:20.370
coming up at the University of Virginia and

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:22.570
the people who go to the graduation parties

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:24.490
are inevitably like the people who live

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:26.570
together first year, right?

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:27.970
I mean, so like

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:30.010
all these students here, right?

00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:32.970
And you know, it's such a dominant factor

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:36.290
like the spatial connections that it's like

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:39.170
college-long friendships and then they come

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:40.170
back for tailgating.

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:42.010
They're like still tailgating with the kids

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:43.050
who they lived

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:45.890
the same dorm hall with.

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:48.570
, which so it's powerful.

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:51.690
So, yeah, I think the companies can

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:53.890
yeah, try to try to make that happen.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:56.480
I just think having worked in big companies

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:58.360
myself for such a long time

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:01.440
getting this right and it's doesn't cost

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:02.440
money. I agree with you.

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:04.000
So many people say, oh, well, we don't

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:06.680
have the budget to change the space.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:08.840
You don't need loads of budget to put

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:09.680
a chair

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:11.600
by the side of the kitchen so people

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:13.240
can sit down or put a couch there.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.600
Like it's not like or move a couch

